Proxy ownKeys: the duplicate check and the target-key lookups through a SORTED COPY (n log n, worst case)

Jahshaka's change, MCP-ON-HOST-1 stage 2 (2026-11-23). quickjs-ng at the pinned
v0.18.0 (d7215b0), file quickjs.c, js_proxy_get_own_property_names.

The cost
--------
A Proxy's ownKeys trap answers a list of n keys; the function then checks it for
duplicates by scanning every earlier key for each key (find_prop_key over
tab[0..i), its own comment: "XXX: inefficient"), and looks each non-configurable
target key up the same way. That is ~n^2/2 comparisons in C, where the runtime's
interrupt handler never runs: a script reaches it with no command at all
(Reflect.ownKeys(new Proxy({}, {ownKeys: () => keys}))). Measured on the
Jahshaka box (Debug, script.host's case 18 before this patch): 32,768 keys 0.25 s,
131,072 keys 3.9 s of CPU (15.6x for 4x the keys); bounded only by the heap
limit - ~0.041 s x (limit in MiB)^2, ~42 s at the MCP host's 32 MiB, ~15 h at a
local host's 1168 MiB - so an outside client could hold the engine's script
thread that long, uncancellable.

The fix
-------
A SORTED COPY of the list's (atom, index) pairs - a heapsort: O(n log n) in the
WORST case, whatever atom values the script chose (an integer-like key's atom is
the integer | 1 << 31, so a script picks atoms: a hash table it could crowd was
this patch's first form and is gone - the final read's crafted keys), no
recursion, nothing allocated but the copy. A duplicate is two equal atoms
adjacent in it (the TypeError's text names no key, so which pair is "first" is
not observable); a non-configurable target key is found by a binary search of
it. The trap's list itself is never reordered: the trap's order is the result's.
The copy (8 bytes a key) is allocated through js_malloc (counted by the memory
limit) and freed on both exits. find_prop_key, its only user gone, is removed.

ITS BOUND (no interrupt poll needed inside it): the heap limit bounds n. At the
MCP host's 32 MiB a trap's distinct keys stop near 430,000 (SCRIPT-IN-ENGINE-1
2b's measure: ~13,400 keys a MiB before the limit refuses). MEASURED (Debug,
case 18 after this patch): 131,072 keys listed through the trap in 0.027 s of
CPU, so ~430,000 keys take ~0.1 s (n log n, INFERRED from that measure) - the
bound at which a cancel reaches it; at a local host's derived 1168 MiB (~15.6 M
keys) ~4.5 s, INFERRED the same way (an outside client's host states 32 MiB).
A cancel reaches the script at the runtime's next interrupt poll after it.

ONE ACCEPTED DIFFERENCE: near a full heap, "out of memory" (the copy refused)
can come where the pin would have answered the duplicate or target-key
TypeError: both fail the call by name; the copy cannot be done in place without
reordering the trap's list.

The laws it keeps (script.host case 18, ours): the result in the trap's order; a
duplicate a TypeError "proxy: duplicate property" (at 3 keys, at index 50,000
repeating index 7, and two different duplicates in one list); a non-configurable
target key the trap leaves out a TypeError, and an extra key on a non-extensible
target a TypeError; the right lists accepted. Its scaling law, in CPU time, for
ordinary keys AND for the crafted families (one low-bit residue, adjacent
residues): 4x the keys under 8x the time.

